## FiveM Royale -- server configuration
##
## Target: Legacy FiveM (standard FXServer Linux artifacts), Ubuntu.
## See DEPLOY.md for the full setup walkthrough.
##
## Two things in here are load-bearing and easy to get wrong, so they are called
## out at the top:
##
##   1. The default `chat` resource is NOT started. This gamemode ships its own
##      chat in br_ui, and two chat systems fighting over the T key is a bad
##      time. If you see a duplicate chat box, something re-enabled it.
##
##   2. spawnmanager and basic-gamemode are NOT started. They would spawn players
##      into the world on join and respawn them on death, both of which the match
##      state machine owns. Leaving them on produces players appearing at random
##      map locations mid-match.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Network
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
endpoint_add_tcp "0.0.0.0:30120"
endpoint_add_udp "0.0.0.0:30120"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Players and sync
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# OneSync is required: this gamemode's roster, storm damage and validation all
# run server-side against every connected player.
set onesync on

# 48 is the FREE OneSync ceiling. Going above it requires a Cfx.re Element Club
# tier (Argentum 64 / Arum 128 / Platinum 2048) attached to your license key --
# and setting it higher WITHOUT one makes the server fail its heartbeat check and
# disappear from the public list entirely. Raise Config.Match.maxPlayers in
# br_lib/config/match.lua to match if you ever change this.
sv_maxclients 48

# Sync rate. 60 is a sensible default; raise only if 48-player load testing shows
# sync lag, and expect CPU cost to scale roughly linearly.
set sv_syncTickRate 60

sv_scriptHookAllowed 0
sv_enforceGameBuild 3095

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Entity lockdown -- WHO IS ALLOWED TO CREATE A NETWORKED ENTITY
#
# ⚠ THIS ONE LINE IS THE WHOLE SETTING. It is a server-wide switch with
#   consequences well beyond the vehicles #193 raised it for, and the mode is
#   NOT SETTLED -- see "the unresolved part" below. Change the value, never the
#   surrounding code.
#
# The four values the PARSER accepts, and nothing else:
#
#   inactive    Clients may create any networked entity. This is the platform
#               default and therefore what this project has always run, whether
#               or not anybody decided it.
#   relaxed     Client-created entities are refused UNLESS the engine's own
#               population placed them -- i.e. ambient traffic, parked cars,
#               patrol and scenario vehicles and ambient peds all survive, and a
#               trainer spawning a vehicle does not.
#   no_dummy    NOT "relaxed but more" -- read the validator before reaching for
#               it. It permits everything a client creates EXCEPT dummy objects,
#               so it is closer to `inactive` than to `relaxed`. The docs
#               describe it as "disables dummy object creation", which is
#               accurate and reads stricter than it is.
#   strict      NO entity may be created by a client. AT ALL.
#
# ⚠ THE DOCS ADVERTISE A FIFTH MODE CALLED `full` AND THE PARSER DOES NOT ACCEPT
#   IT. docs.fivem.net's server-commands table lists `full` where the code reads
#   `no_dummy`; the string was added to the docs alongside the GTAV Enhanced
#   pages and never existed in the parser. Putting it here is an unknown value,
#   not a stricter setting.
#
# ═══ WHAT `strict` COSTS, AND WHY IT IS NOT MERELY "relaxed BUT MORE" ═══
#
# The two modes differ in EXACTLY ONE BRANCH of the platform's entity validator,
# and that branch is the carve-out admitting engine-placed population. Under
# strict it is skipped, so RANDOM_AMBIENT, RANDOM_PARKED, RANDOM_PATROL,
# RANDOM_PERMANENT and RANDOM_SCENARIO are all rejected.
#
# So on this gamemode, strict deletes:
#
#   * every ambient vehicle and every parked car -- which are the ENTIRE vehicle
#     supply under #193's Option A, the option the owner chose;
#   * every ambient ped;
#   * and it does it by letting the client spawn them and then rejecting the
#     clone, so the symptom is traffic that appears and vanishes rather than
#     traffic that never comes.
#
# BR.Config.Ambient's `vehicles = 0.45` and `parked = 1.0` would still be written
# every frame by client/natives.lua and would still do nothing.
#
# ═══ WHAT IS SAFE UNDER ANY MODE, so nobody re-tests it ═══
#
#   * The parachute. Lockdown broke parachutes once and the platform added an
#     explicit carve-out for task attachments; it is NOT gated on the mode, so
#     client/skydive.lua is unaffected by any value here.
#   * Everything this gamemode creates. The Battle Bus and its pilot
#     (client/bus.lua) and every loot prop (client/loot.lua) pass
#     isNetwork = false, and lockdown governs only networked creation.
#   * Control requests. Under strict the platform always refuses
#     REQUEST_CONTROL_EVENT, which would break any code asking for an entity it
#     does not own -- and nothing in this tree does. There is no
#     NetworkRequestControlOfEntity call anywhere in resources/; client/state.lua
#     documents deliberately not calling it.
#   * Player peds, ragdoll and DBNO. The validator skips player entities
#     entirely, and a downed player's ped is never recreated.
#
# What DOES change under relaxed or stricter: engine-created weapon pickups --
# the objects GTA drops when a ped dies armed. This gamemode owns its own loot
# and death boxes (server/loot.lua), so losing them is not a gameplay loss, but
# it is a difference and it is not zero.
#
# ═══ THE MODE IS `relaxed`, AND IT WAS A CHOICE BETWEEN TWO DECISIONS ═══
#
# The owner's decisions on #193 were "enable entity lockdown" and "let's not
# change ambient traffic". Under `strict` those two are INCOMPATIBLE: enabling
# it IS changing ambient traffic, by removing it -- ValidateEntity gates the
# whole random-population carve-out on the mode not being Strict, so ambient
# traffic, parked cars and ambient peds all stop existing. Under Option A those
# parked cars are the entire vehicle supply.
#
# `relaxed` satisfies both, and differs from `strict` in EXACTLY THAT ONE BRANCH
# and nothing else. It keeps the population and still refuses client-created
# entities, which is the property the vehicle allowlist and
# br_core/server/vehicles.lua exist to enforce. The owner chose it on
# 2026-08-21: "Relaxed is good."
#
# DO NOT "TIGHTEN" THIS TO `strict` AS AN IMPROVEMENT. It is not a stricter
# version of the same setting; it is the same setting plus deleting the map's
# traffic. And `full` is not a value -- the docs advertise it, the parser
# rejects it.
#
# ═══ AND IT IS WHY A TRAINER CANNOT SPAWN A CAR HERE ANY MORE ═══
#
# Owner, 2026-08-22 (#202): "I can no longer spawn a vehicle through vMenu."
# That is this line working, not a regression. The platform validates a client's
# clone-create BEFORE raising `entityCreating`, so a refused vehicle is deleted
# without br_core ever seeing it -- which is also why `brvehicles` stays at zero
# while it happens.
#
# THE REPLACEMENT IS `brcar <serverId> [model]`, at the server console. It goes
# through a SERVER-side CreateVehicle, which the same validator admits by its
# creation token rather than by the mode -- so it works here and would work
# under `strict` too. Console only, dev mode only, and only models
# br_lib/config/vehicles.lua tolerates. See docs/running.md.
sv_entityLockdown relaxed

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Identity
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
sv_hostname "FiveM Royale | Battle Royale"
sets sv_projectName "FiveM Royale"
sets sv_projectDesc "48-player battle royale. Drop, loot, outlast the storm."
sets locale "en-US"
sets tags "battle royale, br, pvp, gamemode"

# Get one from https://keymaster.fivem.net -- the server will not start without it.
sv_licenseKey "CHANGEME"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Admin permissions
#
# Most br_* commands are registered restricted, so they need this ACE from a live
# client. Typed at the server console they always work, because console commands
# run as source 0.
#
# Replace the identifier with your own license: find it with `brroster` or in the
# server log on join.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
add_ace group.admin br.admin allow
add_ace group.admin command allow
# add_principal identifier.license:PUT_YOUR_LICENSE_HERE group.admin

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Base resources
#
# Deliberately minimal. Anything that spawns, respawns, or moves a player
# conflicts with the match state machine.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
ensure sessionmanager    # connection handling -- required
ensure hardcap           # enforces sv_maxclients

# VOICE. THIRD-PARTY, NOT IN THIS REPOSITORY, AND YOU MUST INSTALL IT YOURSELF.
# See the "Voice" block further down for the exact commands -- this one line is
# the whole of the config, but the resource has to be on the box first.
#
# Started BEFORE br_core so the proximity rules land on a resource that is
# already up. br_core does not require that -- it re-states them if pma-voice
# starts later, and it boots without it -- but there is no reason to make it
# work for a living.
ensure pma-voice

# SCREENSHOTS ON AN INCIDENT (#34). THIRD-PARTY, NOT IN THIS REPOSITORY, AND
# DELIBERATELY NOT VENDORED -- see docs/security.md for why this one is
# installed rather than copied in, unlike pma-voice above.
#
# COMMENTED OUT BECAUSE IT IS GENUINELY OPTIONAL. Without it, incidents are
# filed exactly as before and simply carry no frames -- a supported state, not a
# degraded one. Uncomment after installing it:
#
#   cd resources/[local] && git clone https://github.com/citizenfx/screenshot-basic
#
# It builds itself on first start using FXServer's own yarn/webpack system
# resources, so those must be present (they ship with cfx-server-data). Confirm
# with `brartifacts` in the server console, which says outright whether it is
# running.
# ensure screenshot-basic

# NOT started, on purpose:
#   chat            -- br_ui provides chat; two systems fight over the T key
#   spawnmanager    -- br_core owns spawning and the warmup pad
#   basic-gamemode  -- would respawn players into the world on death
#   mapmanager      -- nothing to load; we use the vanilla map as-is

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# TUNABLES -- THE DEV/PUBLIC SPLIT, AND THE LINE THAT MUST STAY ABOVE `ensure`.
#
# tunables.cfg is a small file of `set br_*` lines holding the handful of
# values a dev server wants different from the public one: squad size, warmup
# length, the summary screen, autofill, bleed-out. It is gitignored, per host,
# and copied from one of two tracked examples in the repo root:
#
#     cp tunables.dev.cfg.example    tunables.cfg     # dev box
#     cp tunables.public.cfg.example tunables.cfg     # public box
#
# Swapping which one you copied and restarting is the whole feature. Nothing
# else in this file changes between the two servers.
#
# THIS LINE MUST STAY ABOVE THE `ensure br_*` BLOCK BELOW, AND THE FAILURE IF
# IT DOES NOT IS SILENT. The gamemode reads these convars once, while
# br_lib/config/*.lua loads -- and br_core/server/match.lua copies two of them
# (warmupSeconds, endedSeconds) into its own table at that same instant. A
# convar set AFTER the resources have started is set, shows up in the admin
# console's config report, and changes nothing whatsoever about the game.
#
# HOW YOU FIND OUT: br_core prints a `tunables` block on start, one line per
# setting, saying for each whether the live value came from a convar or from
# the default in br_lib/config/match.lua. If you set something and that block
# says "default", this exec line is in the wrong place. br_core also re-reads
# the convars ten seconds after start and prints a loud warning if any of them
# appeared in the meantime, which is exactly what an exec below this block
# looks like.
#
# A MISSING OR EMPTY tunables.cfg IS HARMLESS. Every setting falls back to the
# committed default; FXServer says once that it could not exec the file and
# carries on. Leaving this line out entirely is also fine -- it is what a
# public server that never wants to think about this looks like.
#
# A BAD VALUE IS NOT HARMLESS, ON PURPOSE. `set br_maxSquadSize two` stops the
# server at boot with a banner naming the setting, what you typed and what is
# allowed, rather than starting a server with a squad size of nothing.
#
# Unlike everything under resources/, this file is NOT deployed by
# tools/deploy.sh -- see the CONVARS note further down. tunables.cfg reaches
# the box only when a human copies it there.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
exec "tunables.cfg"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# OUR DISCORD, AND IT IS HERE RATHER THAN IN tunables.cfg ON PURPOSE.
#
# Set it and a player who is kicked or refused at the door is told, on the end
# of the same message, where to come and talk to us about it. Leave it out and
# they see the reason and nothing else -- there is no half-line and no empty
# link.
#
# THE POSITION IS THE POINT. It is the same address on a dev box and a public
# one, so it does not belong in either tunables example: copying one of those
# over tunables.cfg is how a box changes character, and an address that has
# nothing to do with which box this is should not be swept away by that. Below
# the exec and above `ensure br_lib` is the one place that is true of.
#
# It is a full link, path and all -- the code after the slash is the invite. A
# value that is not one stops the server at boot with a banner naming it, the
# same as any other setting in the block above.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# set br_discordUrl "https://discord.gg/abcdefg"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Gamemode
#
# ORDER MATTERS.
#   br_lib   is a file container with no runtime scripts, but must exist and be
#            started before anything referencing '@br_lib/...' loads.
#   br_core  defines the loop registry and scheduler.
#   br_ui    owns the NUI page and NUI focus.
#   br_stats and br_ringmaster are the optional ones, and each declares only
#            br_lib on purpose. br_ringmaster refuses `dependency 'br_core'`
#            so the `restart br_core` ending every deploy cannot take the
#            moderation channel with it; br_stats refuses `dependency 'br_ddb'`
#            so a server with no database still plays. Both degrade to "no live
#            state" rather than to "not running".
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
ensure br_lib
ensure br_core
ensure br_environment    # world state: IPLs + the Cayo Perico lobby island
ensure br_ui
ensure br_loadscreen     # branded loading screen; br_core releases it
ensure br_ddb            # DynamoDB: bans, grants, profiles, stats, incidents
ensure br_stats          # match results, XP, Volts, and the report-reward sweep
ensure br_ringmaster     # optional -- the admin console's game-side half

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Ringmaster (M9). ALL OPTIONAL. With none of these set, br_ringmaster boots,
# says it is off in one line, and does nothing for the rest of the run.
#
# The endpoint is a PRIVATE address reached over the VPC peering link. It must
# never be a public hostname and never go through Cloudflare -- if you find
# yourself typing a domain here, something is wrong with the network setup.
#
# The secret is a real credential. server.cfg is gitignored precisely so lines
# like this one can exist; do not move it into server.cfg.example.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# set br_ringmaster_ingest_url    "http://10.0.0.10:3000/api/ingest"
# set br_ringmaster_ingest_secret "<generate with: openssl rand -hex 24>"
# set br_ringmaster_push_ms       2000
#
# (The angle brackets above are not decoration -- tools/check_secrets.sh treats
#  them as a placeholder marker. Without them this line reads as a committed
#  credential and fails the build, which is exactly what it should do to a real
#  one. It caught this very line when it was first written.)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# br_ddb (M9 Slice 2). ALSO OPTIONAL, and NOT A CREDENTIAL in sight -- the SDK
# finds the EC2 instance role through instance metadata on its own. If you ever
# feel the need to put an access key here, the role is wrong.
#
# Both have working defaults; set them only if your tables live elsewhere.
# `brddb` in the server console proves the box can actually reach DynamoDB.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# set br_ddb_region       "us-east-2"
# set br_ddb_table_prefix "ringmaster-"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# INCIDENT ARTIFACTS (M9, #34). The screenshots attached to a moderation case.
#
# THE BUCKET NAME IS HARD-CODED AND THAT IS DELIBERATE (operator, 2026-08-20):
# "Public access is disabled so you can hard-code that bucket in." It is not a
# credential -- the name grants nothing without one -- and the box reaches S3
# with the same EC2 instance role it reaches DynamoDB with. Set this only if you
# are pointing a test box at a different bucket. The IAM grant is PutObject on
# the `incidents/` prefix and nothing else: the game cannot read a frame back,
# list the bucket, or delete anything.
#
# `br_artifacts_dir` is where a frame waits for the few seconds between landing
# on this box and reaching the bucket. The default is under the OS temp
# directory. It is emptied when br_ddb starts and swept on every capture, and
# br_ddb refuses to take a picture at all once it is over its ceiling -- a full
# disk on the game box is a far worse outcome than a missing screenshot.
#
# NOTHING HERE INSTALLS screenshot-basic. It is a third-party Cfx resource and
# it is NOT vendored into this repository (see docs/security.md). If it is not
# running, incidents simply carry no frames -- which is a normal, supported
# state and is not an error. `brartifacts` in the server console says which
# state you are in.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# set br_artifacts_bucket "royale-incidents-bucket"
# set br_artifacts_dir    "/tmp/br_artifacts"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# VOICE. THE RESOURCE IS VENDORED INTO THIS REPOSITORY AND DEPLOYS WITH IT.
#
# The gamemode no longer drives FiveM's Mumble natives itself. It drives
# pma-voice (MIT, (c) Dillon Skaggs), which owns proximity and the squad radio;
# br_core only tells it our rules. Six rounds of hand-rolled voice preceded that
# decision and four of them shipped a silent 'nearby'.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# NOTHING TO INSTALL. THIS SECTION USED TO CARRY A `git clone`. IT DOES NOT ANY
# MORE, AND RUNNING THE OLD ONE NOW IS A BUG.
#
# pma-voice lives at resources/[voice]/pma-voice IN THIS REPOSITORY, pinned at
# v7.0.2-rc3, and tools/deploy.sh syncs it to resources/[voice]/pma-voice on the
# box -- the same path the hand-installed clone used, so a deploy REPLACES that
# clone rather than adding a second resource beside it. `ensure pma-voice`
# (already in the list above) is unchanged, because a FiveM resource is named by
# its directory and that name did not move.
#
# IF THIS BOX PREDATES THE VENDORING it still has the operator's clone at that
# path. The deploy overwrites the files but cannot remove the clone's `.git`
# (rsync --delete does not delete excluded paths), so `rm -rf` that directory
# once, before the first deploy that carries the vendored copy, and let the
# deploy recreate it. Two directories both called pma-voice -- or one whose
# `git describe` and whose contents disagree -- is the "two implementations,
# nothing asserting only one is active" fault this project has shipped three
# times.
#
# WHY IT IS VENDORED, having deliberately not been for several rounds. Three
# player-facing faults were unreachable from outside the resource: an
# unconditional `print` that put "0.6" in every player's F8 console whenever
# anybody talked; a mic-click chirp with no on/off convar; and two keys
# registered outside our own key layer (F11 "Cycle Proximity", Left Alt "Talk
# over Radio"). Each was reported as "no convar exists, cannot be fixed", which
# was accepting the wrong constraint -- pma-voice is MIT. The local changes are
# four lines, each marked BR-PATCH and logged in
# resources/[voice]/pma-voice/VENDOR.json, which also records the upstream tag
# and commit so the next bump is a re-extract and a re-apply rather than
# archaeology. tools/verify.sh fails the build if that log and the source drift
# apart, if the LICENSE goes missing, or if deploy.sh stops syncing it.
#
# THE PIN MOVED OFF v7.0.0, AND THIS PARAGRAPH USED TO ARGUE THE OPPOSITE.
#
# It said v7.0.0 was "the latest tag that is not a release candidate" and that
# the rcs were "not recommended for a live box". That was a reasonable default
# and it was wrong here, because v7.0.0 has the bug this server is reporting.
#
# v7.0.0, client/init/proximity.lua, inside addNearbyPlayers():
#
#     MumbleAddVoiceChannelListen(playerServerId)
#
# -- listen to our OWN Mumble channel, unconditionally, every 200 ms, started
# by a loop that waits only for `MumbleIsConnected()` and NOT for pma-voice's
# own handleInitialState() to have joined that channel. Until the join lands the
# native has nothing to resolve, and FiveM prints
#
#     MUMBLE_ADD_VOICE_CHANNEL_LISTEN: Tried to call native on a channel that
#     didn't exist
#
# five times a second, from the moment a player connects. That is #165's third
# report, in full. NO CONVAR REACHES IT: the call takes none, and the two convars
# below (which do work, and did close the two OTHER listen sites) cannot help.
#
# v7.0.1-rc2 and v7.0.2-rc3 fix exactly this, upstream, three ways: the loop is
# gated on an `isInitialized` flag that handleInitialState sets only after the
# join converges; the own-channel listen uses a server-assigned
# `assignedChannel` instead of the player's server id; and the other two listen
# sites resolve MumbleGetVoiceChannelFromServerId first and do nothing when it
# is -1, queueing a retry instead of warning.
#
# THE RC LABEL IS UPSTREAM'S RELEASE PROCESS, NOT A STATEMENT ABOUT THIS CODE.
# Every export br_core uses -- overrideProximityCheck, overrideProximityRange,
# setRadioChannel, addChannelCheck, setPlayerRadio, getMutedPlayers,
# toggleMutePlayer -- was checked present in v7.0.2-rc3 as well as v7.0.0, and
# the proximity-check contract (fn(ply) -> shouldAdd, distance) is unchanged.
#
# AND br_core NO LONGER TRUSTS THIS PARAGRAPH TO REACH ANYONE. It reads
# pma-voice's own `assignedChannel` state bag at runtime, which exists from
# v7.0.1-rc2 onwards and does not in v7.0.0, and prints the upgrade command on
# the server console and in `brvoice` on both sides when it finds the old one.
# This file has now been the place a #165 fix went to die twice.
#
# THE DIRECTORY STILL MATTERS AND IT IS STILL NOT ARBITRARY. `[voice]` is a
# sibling of `[gamemodes]`, and tools/deploy.sh's MAIN rsync is --delete scoped
# to resources/[gamemodes]/[fivem-royale]/ only. The vendored copy therefore
# gets a SECOND rsync of its own, with --delete scoped to
# resources/[voice]/pma-voice/ -- inside the one resource directory, never at
# the `[voice]` category level, so another voice resource installed alongside it
# cannot be deleted by our deploy.
#
# WHAT THE OLD VERSION OF THIS FILE TRADED AWAY, recorded because the argument
# was reasonable and still lost:
#   - "We would own its bugs." We already did. Three of them were in front of
#     players for several rounds precisely because we could not reach the source.
#   - "An operator should be able to take an upstream fix the day it lands."
#     Still true, and the cost is now a re-extract of the new tag plus four
#     marked lines to re-apply. VENDOR.json exists to keep that cheap.
#   - "check_secrets.sh would sweep it forever." It does, and it passes.
# br_core still detects the resource at runtime with GetResourceState and still
# boots without it, so a box that somehow has no pma-voice says so on the server
# console at start and on the first line of `brvoice` rather than failing quiet.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CONVARS. TWO ARE REQUIRED -- AND br_core NOW SETS BOTH ITSELF.
#
# READ THIS BEFORE ADDING ANYTHING BELOW. THIS FILE IS NOT A CONFIG FILE.
# server.cfg is in .gitignore and tools/deploy.sh syncs
# resources/[fivem-royale]/ and nothing else, so a line added HERE reaches the
# box only when a human retypes it into the real server.cfg. #165 is what that
# costs: both required convars were added to this file last round, neither was
# ever on the server, and both symptoms were reported again the same week.
#
# So br_core/server/voice.lua now calls SetConvarReplicated for both of them at
# start, and ONLY when server.cfg has not set them. Leaving both lines below
# commented out is a supported, and frankly the recommended, configuration --
# br_core says in the server console which ones it had to set. Setting one
# explicitly to something else keeps YOUR value and prints what it costs.
#
# REQUIRED (1) -- stop a scripted camera turning players into spectators.
#
# pma-voice's proximity loop treats ANY rendering scripted camera as spectating
# (client/init/proximity.lua: `GetRenderingCam() ~= -1`), and a spectator gets
# a Mumble channel listen on every streamed player -- unlimited range, no
# falloff. This gamemode renders a scripted camera in the lobby
# (br_core/client/lobbycam.lua), ON THE DROP BUS (client/bus.lua) and while
# downed (client/dbno.lua), so with this at its default of 0 every rider spends
# the flight listening to the whole plane.
#
# IT ALSO LEAKS. The listens are taken over the WARMUP bucket's player list and
# dropped over the MATCH bucket's a flight later, so whatever was in the first
# and not the second is never removed -- an unlimited-range listen on players
# in other matches, for the rest of the session.
#
# WHAT THIS BLOCK USED TO CLAIM AND SHOULD NOT HAVE, TWICE OVER. It said this
# convar was where #165's
#     MUMBLE_ADD_VOICE_CHANNEL_LISTEN: Tried to call native on a channel that
#     didn't exist
# came from, and then that the remainder was pma-voice's version. Neither was
# right. THE WARNING WAS vMENU'S OWN VOICE CHAT (#185) -- a second voice
# implementation, on by default, that ran alongside pma-voice through the whole
# of #150, #157 and #165 while nobody had enumerated what else on this box
# touches voice. Turn vMenu's voice off; the console is clean with it off.
# Setting this convar is still required, for the leak described above and not
# for any warning.
# setr voice_disableAutomaticListenerOnCamera 1
#
# REQUIRED (2) -- turn pma-voice's own overlay off.
#
# This is the bottom-RIGHT readout: "Custom [Range]", and "[Call]" and
# "N Mhz [Radio]" alongside it. All three are one `v-if="voice.uiEnabled"` in
# pma-voice's voice-ui/src/App.vue, and `uiEnabled` is this convar, sent to its
# NUI once at start from client/init/init.lua. The gamemode draws its own
# indicator (bottom CENTRE, driven by br_core/client/voice.lua), and running
# both is the second contradicting voice this config already complains about
# elsewhere.
#
# WRITTEN DOWN BECAUSE THIS FILE PREVIOUSLY GOT IT WRONG IN BOTH DIRECTIONS. It
# was first recommended while no voice resource was installed, where it did
# nothing at all, and this file then said it "DOES NOTHING ON THIS SERVER" --
# true at the time, false the moment pma-voice is ensured.
#
# IF YOU SET IT AT RUNTIME rather than in server.cfg, `restart pma-voice`
# afterwards: the value is read once, when pma-voice's client script starts.
# setr voice_enableUi 0
#
# RECOMMENDED -- stop F11 cycling the proximity range.
#
# pma-voice ships three cycleable ranges (whisper/normal/shout) and a key to
# rotate them. This gamemode has ONE range, from Config.Match.voice.range.nearby.
# br_core already disables the cycle through the overrideProximityRange export;
# this is the belt to those braces.
#
# IT DOES NOT REMOVE THE KEYBIND, and an earlier version of this comment claimed
# it did. Upstream registers the F11 mapping unconditionally, so with this
# convar at 0 the row stayed in the player's key list and did nothing when
# pressed -- which is indistinguishable from broken. BR-PATCH 3a in the vendored
# copy removes the RegisterKeyMapping and leaves the command; that is what
# actually takes the row out of the list.
setr voice_enableProximityCycle 0
#
# REQUIRED (3) -- no hold-a-radio animation while squadmates talk.
#
# pma-voice's radio push-to-talk plays 'random@arrests'/'generic_radio_enter'
# on the talker's ped for as long as the key is held. That is the right default
# for a roleplay server and wrong twice here: it re-poses a player who is
# aiming a rifle, and it is a visible tell to every enemy who can see them that
# they are on comms. It is purely cosmetic -- no audio goes with it.
# br_core sets this; it is here so you know it exists.
# setr voice_enableRadioAnim 0
#
# REQUIRED (4) -- no chirp when a player presses or releases push-to-talk.
#
# Owner, from the playtest: "It still makes a radio chirp sound when pressing
# and releasing the button ... the button chirp needs to go."
#
# pma-voice plays mic_click_on.ogg / mic_click_off.ogg from its own NUI on the
# radio press and release. UPSTREAM HAS NO ON/OFF CONVAR FOR IT -- the switch is
# a per-client KVP (`pma-voice_enableMicClicks`) reachable only through the
# `setVoiceProperty` export, which br_core/client/voice.lua calls.
#
# THE ACTUAL FIX IS NOW BR-PATCH 2 IN THE VENDORED COPY: playMicClicks() returns
# immediately, so no call site can play a click whatever the KVP, the export or
# these volumes say. The export call and these two convars are kept as the
# backstop for a box still running an unvendored pma-voice -- belt, braces, and
# a second pair of braces. These two are VOLUMES: both feed `click.volume` on
# the <audio> element directly (pma-voice voice-ui/src/App.vue), so zero is
# silence.
#
# br_core sets both; they are here so you know they exist. They are read ONCE,
# at pma-voice's script start, into a table -- so if you set them at runtime,
# `restart pma-voice` afterwards. The export does not have that limitation,
# which is exactly why br_core uses it as well.
# setr voice_onClickVolume 0
# setr voice_offClickVolume 0
#
# DO NOT SET voice_enableSubmix 0. THAT IS A DIFFERENT FEATURE AND THE OWNER
# ASKED TO KEEP IT: "I don't hate the radio sound effect". The submix is the
# processed, over-the-air quality a squadmate's voice takes on while they are
# on the radio (SetAudioSubmixEffectRadioFx, pma-voice client/init/main.lua).
# It has nothing to do with the chirp. It defaults to 1 and nothing in the
# gamemode touches it.
#
# ============================================================================
# SQUAD VOICE IS A RADIO, AND A RADIO HAS ITS OWN KEY -- BUT THE KEY IS OURS
# NOW. READ THIS BEFORE REPORTING THAT SQUAD VOICE DOES NOT WORK.
#
# The gamemode's `squad` voice mode IS a pma-voice radio channel, assigned by
# br_core/server/voice.lua from matchId and squadId. pma-voice transmits on a
# radio ONLY while `+radiotalk` is held (client/module/radio.lua), and until
# this round the only thing that held it was pma-voice's OWN keybind, "Talk
# over Radio", on LEFT ALT -- a default this project had simply never set.
#
# THAT IS NO LONGER THE PLAYER-FACING KEY. br_core registers its own binding,
#
#     brptt, "Royale: Push to talk", default N
#     (br_core/client/keybinds.lua)
#
# and drives BOTH transmit paths from it: `+radiotalk` in squad mode, and GTA's
# INPUT_PUSH_TO_TALK (control 249) in nearby mode, which is what opens the
# microphone for proximity. It is rebindable on the gamemode's own settings
# screen under Controls, Comms, like every other key in this game, and every
# surface that names a key -- the start-of-match notice, the settings screen,
# /brvoice -- reads that binding rather than a convar.
#
# N because 249's own default keyboard binding is N, so on a fresh client the
# gamemode's key and the engine's are the same key.
#
# DO NOT SET voice_enableRadios 0 AND DO NOT NEUTRALISE THE +radiotalk COMMAND.
# The KEY moved; the command did not. It is still the only thing that ever puts
# a squadmate in the Mumble voice target, and br_core calls it. Suppressing it
# makes squad voice silent by construction -- that is #157 in one line.
#
# voice_defaultRadio NOW READS NOTHING AT ALL and is deliberately left alone.
# It was the default for pma-voice's own "Talk over Radio" row on Left Alt --
# owner, verbatim: "Why is left alt used for anything? That was a mistake - a
# default left in place." Setting it to N would only have MOVED that row, and a
# second binding our settings screen cannot list or move is the problem rather
# than the key it sits on. So BR-PATCH 3b removes the RegisterKeyMapping in the
# vendored copy: there is no "Talk over Radio" row any more, and this convar has
# nothing left to move.
#
# A PLAYER WHO ALREADY REBOUND "Talk over Radio" keeps that binding stored in
# their own FiveM profile. It is inert -- nothing registers the mapping now --
# but it will not disappear from their file, so it is not evidence of anything.
# (previously: setr voice_defaultRadio LMENU)
#
# ============================================================================
# pma-voice USED TO PRINT "0.6" IN THE CLIENT CONSOLE. FIXED AT SOURCE, IN THE
# VENDORED COPY. There is still no convar for it -- there is no longer a need
# for one.
#
#     client/init/main.lua, in toggleVoice(), upstream:
#         print(volumes[moduleType])
#
# A bare, unconditional debug print -- not behind logger.info, not behind
# logger.verbose, and NOT behind voice_debugMode. It fired once every time a
# squadmate's radio voice was activated, and the number was
# `voice_defaultRadioVolume / 100`, which is 60/100 = 0.6 at the default.
#
# It is present in v7.0.0, v7.0.1-rc2, v7.0.2-rc3 and on upstream master as of
# 2026-08-19, and no setting silences it -- voice_defaultRadioVolume changes
# WHICH number is printed, not whether it prints. THIS PARAGRAPH USED TO END
# "leave it ... NOT a local edit to pma-voice's files, which are re-cloned on
# every install." Both halves of that stopped being true when the resource was
# vendored: the files are ours to edit and nothing re-clones them.
#
# BR-PATCH 1 replaces the print with logger.verbose, which is pma-voice's own
# logger and is gated on voice_debugMode. See
# resources/[voice]/pma-voice/VENDOR.json.
#
# ============================================================================
# AND ENUMERATE WHAT ELSE ON THIS BOX DOES VOICE. THIS IS #185.
#
# vMenu ships its own voice chat, ON BY DEFAULT, and it ran alongside pma-voice
# for the whole of #150, #157 and #165. Every measurement taken in that time
# describes the pair. TURN vMENU'S VOICE OFF and leave it off.
#
# br_core now reports this itself: it names any other started resource it
# recognises as a voice implementation, in the server console at start and on
# the `others` line of `brvoice`. If a fourth one turns up, add it to
# VOICE_SUSPECTS in br_core/server/voice.lua rather than finding out in a
# playtest.
# ============================================================================
#
# OPTIONAL -- native audio playback. STILL RECOMMENDED, STILL SECOND.
#
#   OFF (default, what you get if you leave this alone)
#     Voices are mixed by Mumble. Proximity still fades with distance -- that
#     is pma-voice's doing and it is the capability this whole change was made
#     to get -- but the sound is not placed in the world by the game engine.
#
#   ON
#     Playback is handed to GTA's own audio engine: occlusion, reverb, and a
#     voice that sounds like it is coming from where the player is standing.
#
#     ONE THING TO KNOW BEFORE YOU TURN IT ON. pma-voice multiplies its own
#     proximity range by three when this convar is set (client/init/proximity.lua).
#     br_core does NOT apply that multiplier -- our range is stated in world
#     metres and our proximity check compares metres -- so the CUTOFF does not
#     move when you flip this. What moves is how the last few metres sound.
#     (An earlier version of this note claimed the same outcome for the
#      hand-rolled system, which was true then for a different reason. It is
#      restated here because the reason changed and the conclusion did not.)
#
# TURN IT ON SECOND, after you have confirmed the range works. A hard edge is
# unambiguous; a fade and a broken cutoff can sound alike.
#
# setr voice_useNativeAudio true
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHAT DOES *NOT* GO HERE.
#
# The proximity range itself. It is the gamemode's, from
# br_lib/config/match.lua (Config.Match.voice.range.nearby), pushed to each
# client and handed to pma-voice by br_core. Do not set voice_defaultVoiceMode
# or add voice modes -- br_core overrides the range on every match assignment
# and would overwrite you.
#
# The squad radio channel numbers. Also the gamemode's, derived from matchId and
# squadId in br_core/server/voice.lua, and gated by a pma-voice addChannelCheck
# so a client cannot join a squad it is not on.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# IF VOICE IS BROKEN, IN ORDER:
#
#   1. `brvoice` in the SERVER console. First line says whether pma-voice is
#      running at all; the `convar` lines under it say whether it is configured.
#      It lists squad radios only -- proximity is not assigned by the server any
#      more and cannot be diagnosed from there.
#   2. F8 `brvoice` on the affected CLIENT. First line, again, is the engine.
#      A `convars` line appears only when one is wrong ON THIS CLIENT, which is
#      the claim that matters -- both are read client-side. Then "transmitting".
#   3. AND THIS IS THE PART THAT IS NEW: proximity is enforced by the SPEAKER.
#      If A cannot hear B, the machine to run /brvoice on is B'S, not A's.
#      There is nothing on A's client that can be refusing B.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Our loading screen draws its own progress; the engine's busy spinner over
# it is just a second, contradicting voice.
setr sv_showBusySpinnerOnLoadingScreen false

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Third-party admin tools (vMenu etc.)
#
# If you run vMenu alongside this gamemode, DISABLE its overlapping features --
# the gamemode owns them and the two will fight:
#   - Player blips        (gamemode shows squadmates only)
#   - Overhead names      (gamemode shows squadmates only)
#   - Join/quit notifications (the gamemode hides GTA's feed entirely)
#   - PvP / friendly fire settings (the gamemode forces PvP on)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Development
#
# sv_devMode enables client dev tools and lowers the minimum players to start,
# so a match can be tested with two people instead of sixteen.
#
# TURN THIS OFF FOR PRODUCTION.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
set sv_devMode true
set br_devMode true

# Useful console commands once running:
#   brhelp        list every server command
#   brstate       match state, counts, timers
#   brroster      every player the server knows about
#   brstorm       storm record + live containment check
#   brwhy <id>    why a given player is in the state they are in
#   brperf        scheduler cost per job
#   brdb          database health
#   brtop         leaderboard
#
# On a client, in the F8 console:
#   brnativecheck verify native assumptions against this build  <-- run this first
#   brdebug       on-screen overlay (perf / state / keys)
#   brperf        client loop cost per subsystem
